# LandingOS — environment variables template.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in the values before starting the dev
# server:
#
#   cp .env.example .env        (Linux / macOS)
#   copy .env.example .env      (Windows CMD)
#
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DATABASE_URL
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SQLite database file path. The path is relative to the prisma/ directory
# (where schema.prisma lives), so "file:./db/custom.db" resolves to
# <project>/prisma/db/custom.db. This is portable across Windows, Linux, and
# macOS — no absolute paths.
#
# For production, switch the Prisma provider to "postgresql" in
# prisma/schema.prisma and set DATABASE_URL to a Postgres connection string.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
DATABASE_URL="file:./db/custom.db"

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# AUTH_SECRET
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# HMAC secret for signing admin session JWTs. REQUIRED for authentication.
# Without it the login endpoint returns HTTP 500 AUTH_SECRET_MISSING.
#
# Generate a secure random value with one of:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
#
# Never commit the real .env file.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
AUTH_SECRET=

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ADMIN_USERNAME / ADMIN_PASSWORD  (optional, used by the database seed)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Credentials for the seeded admin account. Used only on first boot when the
# database is created. Defaults to admin / admin123 — fine for local preview,
# but for a PUBLIC deployment set a strong, unique password here.
#
# If ADMIN_PASSWORD is set to anything other than "admin123", the account is
# created with mustChangePassword=false so you can log straight in.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
ADMIN_USERNAME=admin
ADMIN_PASSWORD=admin123

# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CLOUDFLARE R2  (image storage — strongly recommended in production)
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where uploaded product images are stored.
#
# Leave these BLANK for local development: uploads fall back to the local
# directory (data/uploads) and everything works with no cloud account.
#
# SET THEM in production on any host with an ephemeral filesystem — including
# Render's free tier, where the container is wiped on every deploy AND every
# time the instance spins down after inactivity. Without R2 there, uploaded
# images disappear within hours.
#
# Where to find each value in the Cloudflare dashboard (R2 → Overview):
#   R2_ACCOUNT_ID        Account ID shown on the R2 overview page
#   R2_BUCKET            The bucket name you created
#   R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID     R2 → Manage API Tokens → Create token (Object
#   R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY Read & Write). Both values are shown ONCE — copy them.
#
# R2_PUBLIC_BASE_URL is OPTIONAL:
#   - Left blank, the bucket stays private and images are proxied through the
#     app. Simplest setup, works immediately.
#   - Set to a public bucket URL (Settings → Public Development URL, looks
#     like https://pub-xxxx.r2.dev) or a custom domain, image requests are
#     redirected straight to Cloudflare's CDN — faster, and image traffic no
#     longer counts against the app host's bandwidth.
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
R2_ACCOUNT_ID=
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
R2_BUCKET=
R2_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=

# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# PRODUCTION DEPLOY (Railway / Render / Fly.io — Docker-based hosts)
# ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# The Dockerfile runs the app as a Next.js standalone server and persists the
# SQLite DB + uploaded images to a mounted volume. Set these on your host:
#
#   AUTH_SECRET       — a random 32+ byte string (generate with the command above)
#   ADMIN_USERNAME    — your chosen admin username (e.g. "admin")
#   ADMIN_PASSWORD    — a strong password for the admin account
#   PORT              — the container port (default 3000; the host sets this)
#   DATABASE_URL      — DO NOT set this; the Dockerfile points it at the volume
#   R2_*              — see the Cloudflare R2 section above. Required on hosts
#                       without a persistent disk (e.g. Render free tier),
#                       otherwise uploaded images are lost on every restart.
#
# On Railway: create a "Volume" and mount it at /app/data so the SQLite file
# and uploads survive redeploys. Railway auto-generates a public HTTPS URL.
#
# NOTE: a volume/disk only covers the SQLite DATABASE. Image uploads are a
# separate concern — with R2 configured they live in object storage instead,
# which is what makes them survive on hosts with no disk at all.

